-- title:
--   Existence of the Atkin–Lehner automorphism wₚ at level Np
-- statement:
--   Let $N$ and $p$ be nonzero natural numbers with $p$ prime and $p \nmid N$. Inside the field $\mathbb{Q}((q))$ of Laurent series over $\mathbb{Q}$, write $j(q^d)$ for the image `qExpand ℚ d jq` of the $q$-expansion `jq` of the $j$-function under the ring homomorphism that multiplies exponents by $d$, and let `modularFunctionFieldFull M` be the intermediate field of $\mathbb{Q}((q))/\mathbb{Q}$ generated by the set of all $j(q^d)$ with $d$ a nonzero divisor of $M$. The theorem asserts that there exists a $\mathbb{Q}$-algebra automorphism $\sigma$ of `modularFunctionFieldFull (N * p)` satisfying the predicate `IsAtkinLehnerAutFull N p`, that is: for every nonzero $d$ dividing $N$, $\sigma$ sends $j(q^{d})$ to $j(q^{dp})$ and $j(q^{dp})$ to $j(q^{d})$, both elements being taken in `modularFunctionFieldFull (N * p)` via the divisibilities $d \mid Np$ and $dp \mid Np$. Since $p \nmid N$, every divisor of $Np$ is of the form $d$ or $dp$ with $d \mid N$, so these conditions prescribe $\sigma$ on a generating set; the statement is existence only, and no uniqueness or involutivity of $\sigma$ is asserted.
--
--   This is the partial Atkin–Lehner involution $w_p$ on the function field of $X_0(Np)$ for $p$ prime not dividing $N$, in the guise of its action on the $q$-expansions $j(q^d)$, $d \mid Np$. It generalises the prime-level case and is used throughout the subsequent study of models of modular curves, in particular in the analysis of the fibres and cusps of $X_0(Np)$ in characteristic $p$.
